Here is a C++ function to calculate the great circle distance between two points on the Earth, accounting for curvature:
```cpp
double earthDistance(double lat1, double lon1, double lat2, double lon2) {
double radius = 3959; // miles
double dLat = (lat2 - lat1) * M_PI / 180;
double dLon = (lon2 - lon1) * M_PI / 180;
double a = sin(dLat/2) * sin(dLat/2) +
cos(lat1 * M_PI / 180) * cos(lat2 * M_PI / 180) *
sin(dLon/2) * sin(dLon/2);
double c = 2 * atan2(sqrt(a), sqrt(1-a));
return radius * c;
}
```
This uses the haversine formula to calculate the great circle distance between the two points specified by latitude and longitude. The Earth's radius is set to 3959 miles. You would call the function passing in the latitude and longitude of the two points in degrees.

